      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we start by taking the square root of both sides. This gives us two possible equations:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = \\sqrt{9} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -\\sqrt{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{9} = 3\\) and \\(-\\sqrt{9} = -3\\), we can rewrite the equations as:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve each of these equations for \\(x\\):\n\n1. For \\(x - 5 = 3\\):\n\\[\nx = 3 + 5 = 8\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(x - 5 = -3\\):\n\\[\nx = -3 + 5 = 2\n\\]\n\nThe roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). To find the sum of the roots, we add these solutions together:\n\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
